    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d521">

                <pb facs="0706" n="420" xml:id="pg420"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 763.72112/2747</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Great Britain</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Page</hi>)
                        <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">London</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1916-07-25T13:00:00+01:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">July 25, 1916, 1 p. m.</hi>
                        </date>
                        <lb/> [<hi rendition="#italic">Received 5.10 p. m.</hi>]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>4606. Your 3552,<anchor n="1" xml:id="d521-f1"/> July 21, 1 [8] p. m. In
                    discussing the black list again with Lord Robert
                        Cecil, I suggested that the British Government authorize some of
                    its officials in the United States to receive statements by the blacklisted and
                    to have authority to remove names from the list when good reason for removal is
                    shown. He thought that this suggestion could be carried into effect, and he
                    promised to make an early statement on the subject in the House of Commons. I
                    emphasized to him and Lord Crewe together the most unfortunate effect for them
                    as well as for us that the list is having in the United States. European
                    neutrals also as well as Britannic business interests are remonstrating. I think
                    they see they have made a bad tactical error and I expect a gradual correction
                    of it.</p>
                <p>They have had such a list for a year in China and for a shorter time in South
                    America where they say [it] has worked advantageously for them. I reminded them
                    that this gave no reasons for applying it to the United States.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Page</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
